  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<table><thead><tr><th><div>Globulin Level</div></th><th><div>Interpretation</div></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><div>Normal Range (Typically 1.8-3.6 g/dL)</div></td><td><div>Indicates normal globulin levels in the blood</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Elevated Levels</div></td><td><div>High globulin levels may be seen in conditions such as chronic inflammation, liver disease, autoimmune disorders, infections, multiple myeloma, and certain cancers. Further evaluation is necessary to determine the underlying cause.</div></td></tr><tr><td><div>Low Levels</div></td><td><div>Low globulin levels can occur in conditions such as severe malnutrition, liver disease, kidney disorders, or genetic disorders. Additional testing is required to identify the underlying cause.</div></td></tr></tbody></table>",
